# Line-ending policy.
#
# AID ships POSIX shell scripts that adopters run via bash, and run_generator.py
# always writes LF. Forcing LF for shell/JS/Python text prevents a contributor
# whose git core.autocrlf=true (this repo is authored on Windows) from committing
# CRLF that would break `#!/usr/bin/env bash` on Linux/macOS adopters — and would
# break the byte-exact render-drift CI gate.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
# Shared payloads are copied and hashed verbatim; match their installed LF policy.
# Auto detection preserves binary assets within these trees.
canonical/aid/**    text=auto eol=lf
canonical/skills/** text=auto eol=lf
bin/aid text eol=lf
# Markdown must be LF too: build-kb-index.sh's awk frontmatter parser chokes on
# CRLF on Linux (intent: blocks parse as "no intent declared"), which broke the
# kb-hygiene CI check.
*.md   text eol=lf
# The dashboard file-set manifest is extensionless (no rule above covers it) and is
# line-parsed by install.sh / install.ps1 / vendor.js / vendor.py / release.sh. Force LF
# so a Windows autocrlf checkout can't inject CRLF that a consumer's parser mishandles
# (a CRLF checkout made vendor.js leak comment lines as bogus file paths).
dashboard/MANIFEST text eol=lf
# The per-tool install descriptor is extensionless too, and release.sh copies it into
# each tool artifact verbatim. The CLI refuses a descriptor that is not ASCII with LF
# endings, so a Windows autocrlf checkout that stages a release made every `aid add`
# from that stage fail.
profiles/*/aid-tool.conf text eol=lf
# Two registered files that sit outside every root above and that no extension rule
# reaches: the settings template (verbatim class) and the generated-file registry
# (transformed class).
canonical/aid/templates/settings.yml         text eol=lf
canonical/aid/templates/generated-files.txt  text eol=lf
# The install manifest is LF-only in history but had no rule, so a Windows tool that
# rewrote it (Python's default newline translation) silently committed CRLF —
# functionally harmless, since every consumer JSON-parses it, but it turned a 72-line
# path deletion into an 875-line whole-file re-encode and made the diff of the very
# artifact under review unreadable. Scoped to this one file deliberately rather than a
# blanket *.json rule: .claude/settings.json has been CRLF since before this policy and
# renormalising it is a separate change.
.aid/.aid-manifest.json text eol=lf

